## Phase 3 — 10yr Yield Direction (Up/Down)

**Question:** Can macro data predict whether the 10yr yield rises or falls next month?

**Results (walk-forward, n=51 test months, 2020–2024):**
| Model | Accuracy | vs Majority-Class | McNemar p |
|-------|----------|------------------|----------|
| Majority class (always UP) | 60.8% | baseline | — |
| Logit | 60.8% | **+0pp** | p=0.71 ✗ |
| RF | 54.9% | -5.9pp (worse) | — |

**Verdict: NO EDGE**

**Critical corrections from initial analysis:**
- Initial report claimed "Logit +12pp vs naive (49%)" — this was misleading
- The naive baseline (last direction = next) = 49% is BELOW majority-class (60.8%)
- Correct comparison: Logit 60.8% = majority-class baseline exactly — zero improvement
- COVID era 68% accuracy is explained by the sustained rate-hike trend (always-UP would also score ~59% in that period; Logit adds only +9pp)

**Logit confusion matrix reveals UP-bias:**
- Predicts DOWN only 20/51 times
- DOWN recall: 65% (better than expected)
- UP recall: 58% (worse than expected)
- The model is not learning yield direction — it's learning the rate-hike regime

**Data limitation:** ^TNX only available from 2019-08 on Yahoo Finance. Test set ends 2024-04 — missing 20 months of recent rate-cut cycle data.

**Why yield direction is hard:**
- Fed forward guidance dominates (not in any free dataset)
- Geopolitical shocks (not predictable)
- QE/QT distorts the yield-macro relationship
- Monthly data is too coarse — yield moves happen on specific announcement days

